<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=soap">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Soap</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2800 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/29th-century-bce">29th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Soap is a salt of a fatty acid, often made by mixing fats or oils with an alkali, used for washing and cleaning; the earliest recorded evidence of soap-like materials comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, c. 2800 BCE. A Sumerian clay tablet of c. 2500 BCE gives a formula of oil and wood ash for washing woollen clothing. These proto-soaps differed from true soap, which foams; who first made true soap is uncertain, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> called it &quot;an invention of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>&quot;. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-golden-age">Islamic Golden Age</a> soap-making became an established industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/soap.jpg" alt="Soap" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DimiTalen · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bars_of_pure_Marseille_and_Aleppo_soap,_2024.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 2800 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Mesopotamia</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Who first made true soap, as distinct from earlier soap-like mixtures, is uncertain; Pliny the Elder called it an invention of the Gauls.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=soap">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
